<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-sets-set-operations" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
	<title>Sets and Set Operations</title>

	<objectives>
		<ul>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use set notation and understand the empty set.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Determine the universal set for a given context.
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Perform basic set operations (union, intersection, and complement).
				</p>
			</li>
			<li>
				<p>
					Use Venn diagrams and set notation to illustrate the intersection, union, and complements of sets.
				</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
	</objectives>

	<introduction>
		<p>
			The idea of a <term>set</term> allows us to treat a group of distinct objects as a single entity. For example, we could use <m>S</m> to represent the four seasons: spring, summer, fall, and winter.  That is,
			<md>
				S = \left\{\text{spring, summer, fall, winter} \right\}.
			</md>
			By classifying items as groups (sets), we can also investigate relationships between groups, or consider overlap between groups when counting items.
		</p>
		<definition>
			<statement>
				<p>
					A <term>set</term><idx>sets</idx> is an unordered collection of distinct objects, called the <term>elements</term><idx><h>sets</h><h>element</h></idx> (or <term>members</term><idx><h>member (of a set)</h><see>sets, element</see></idx>) of the set.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
	</introduction>

	<subsection>
		<title>Set Notation</title>
		<p>
			A set can be defined by describing its contents or by listing its elements. Sets are usually named with capital letters, and braces { } are used to enclose the set's elements. 
			The method of listing the elements of a set in braces is called <term>roster notation</term><idx>roster notation</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>roster notation</h></idx>. A second method uses <term>set-builder notation</term><idx>set-builder notation</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>set-builder notation</h></idx>, where braces and a vertical line | (read <q>such that</q>) describe the set's contents.
		</p>
		<p>
			To say that an object <m>x</m> is an element of a set <m>A</m>, we write <m>x \in A</m>; if it is not, we write <m>x \notin A</m>. For example, <m>4 \in \{0,2,4,6,8\}</m>, but <m>5 \notin \{0,2,4,6,8\}</m>.
		</p>
		<example>
			<p>
				Describing a set by its contents:
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							The even digits.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							The colors of the rainbow.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
			<p>
				Roster notation:
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>E = \{0,2,4,6,8\}</m>
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>C = \{\text{red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet} \}</m>
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
			<p>
				Set-builder notation:
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>E = \{x \mid x \text{ is an even digit}\}</m>
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							<m>C = \{x \mid x \text{ is a color of the rainbow}\}</m>
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
		</example>
		<example>
			<statement>
			<p>
				Julia, Keenan, Jae, and Colin took a test. They received the following scores: 70, 95, 85, and 70. Let <m>P</m> be the set of test takers and <m>S</m> be the set of test scores. List the elements of each set using roster notation.
			</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					In this example, the set of people taking the test is <m>P= \{\text{Julia, Keenan, Jae, Colin}\}</m>, and the set of test scores is <m>S = \{70, 85, 95\}</m>. Notice that even though two people scored a 70 on the test, the score of 70 is listed only once, as elements in a set are unique.
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<p>
			Sometimes a collection might not contain all the elements of a set.  For example, suppose Chris owns three Taylor Swift albums.  While Chris's collection is a set, we can also say it is a subset of the larger set of all Taylor Swift albums.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-subset">
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be two sets. Then <m>A</m> is a <term>subset</term><idx>subset</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>subset</h></idx> of <m>B</m>, written <m>A\subseteq B</m>, if and only if every element of <m>A</m> is also an element of <m>B</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<definition>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> be two sets. We say that <m>A</m> is <term>equal</term><idx><h>sets</h><h>equal</h></idx> to <m>B</m>, written <m>A = B</m>, if and only if <m>A\subseteq B</m> and <m>B\subseteq A</m>. This means that <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> contain the same elements.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<example>
			<p>
				Consider the three sets
				<md>
					A = \{1,2,3\},\quad B = \{1,2,3,4,5\},\quad C = \{3,2,1\}.
				</md>
				Then each of the following set relationships is true:
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p><m>A \subseteq B</m></p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><m>A = C</m></p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p><m>A \subseteq C</m></p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
		</example>
		<p>
			It is possible to have a set with nothing in it. This set is called the <term>null set</term><idx><h>null set</h><see>empty set</see></idx> or <term>empty set</term>. It is like going to the grocery store to buy your favorite foods and realizing you left your wallet at home. 
			You walk away with an empty bag. The set of items that you bought at the grocery store would be written in set notation as <m>\emptyset</m>. The empty set is a subset of every set.
		</p>
		<definition>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The set that contains no elements is called the <term>empty set</term><idx>empty set</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>empty set</h></idx> and is denoted <m>\emptyset</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<p>
			In applications of sets, the elements that may be considered are often limited to some context, or <term>universe of discourse</term><idx><h>universe of discourse</h><see>universal set</see></idx>. This context is called the <term>universal set</term> and is the set containing every possible element of the described context. Every set is thus a subset of the universal set.
		</p>
		<p>
			When represented visually, the universal set is often illustrated by a rectangle labeled with a capital letter <m>U</m>. Subsets of the universal set are usually illustrated with circles for simplicity, but other shapes can be used. The visual representation of sets is further discussed in <xref text="title" ref="subsec-sets-venn-diagrams"/>.
		</p>
		<definition>
			<statement>
				<p>
					The <term>universal set</term><idx>universal set</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>universal set</h></idx> <m>U</m> is defined to be the set of all elements under consideration.
				</p>
			</statement>
		</definition>
		<example>
			<p>
				What the universal set contains depends on context and is sometimes subject to interpretation. Here are some examples.
			</p>
			<p>
			<ol marker="a)">
				<li>
					<sidebyside valign="middle" width="40%">
						<p>
							If you are searching for books for a research project, the universal set might be all the books in the library, and the books in the library that are relevant to your research project would be a subset of the universal set.
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-set-ex1" width="100%">
							<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U, labeled all books in the library; there is a circle inside the rectangle, labeled books relevant to your research project.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
									% Define the universe as a rectangle
									\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (6,4);

									% Add label for the universe inside the box
									\node[align=center] at (4.65,2) {U = All books \\ in the library};

									% Draw the yellow circle for research-relevant books
									\filldraw[fill=yellow!70, draw=black, thick] (1.75,2) circle (1.5);

									% Add label for the research books - on four lines
									\node[align=center] at (1.75,2) {Books \\ relevant to \\ your research \\ project};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</sidebyside>
				</li>
				<li>
					<sidebyside valign="middle" width="40%">
						<p>
							If you want to create a group of your Facebook friends who are coworkers, the universal set would be all your Facebook friends and the group of coworkers would be a subset of the universal set.
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-set-ex2" width="100%">
							<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U, labeled "All your Facebook friends"; there is a circle inside the rectangle, labeled "Coworkers".</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
									% Define the universe as a rectangle
									\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (6,4);
							
									% Add label for the universe inside the box
									\node[align=center] at (4.65,2) {U = All your \\ Facebook \\ friends};
							
									% Draw the yellow circle for the subset
									\filldraw[fill=yellow!70, draw=black, thick] (1.75,2) circle (1.5);
							
									% Add label for the subset
									\node[align=center] at (1.75,2) {Coworkers};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</sidebyside>
				</li>
				<li>
					<sidebyside valign="middle" width="40%">
						<p>
							If you are working with sets of numbers, the universal set might be all whole numbers, and the set of all prime numbers would be a subset of the universal set.
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-set-ex3" width="100%">
							<shortdescription>A rectangle is drawn for the universal set U, labeled "All whole numbers"; there is a circle inside the rectangle, labeled "All prime numbers".</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
									% Define the universe as a rectangle
									\draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (6,4);
							
									% Add label for the universe inside the box
									\node[align=center] at (4.65,2) {U = All whole \\ numbers};
							
									% Draw the yellow circle for the subset
									\filldraw[fill=yellow!70, draw=black, thick] (1.75,2) circle (1.5);
							
									% Add label for the subset
									\node[align=center] at (1.75,2) {All prime \\numbers};
								\end{tikzpicture}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</sidebyside>
				</li>
			</ol>
		</p>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec-set-operations">
		<title>Set Operations: Union, Intersection, and Complement</title>
		<p>
			Suppose you and your roommate decide to have a house party, and you each invite your circle, or set, of friends. When you combine your two sets of friends, you discover that you have some friends in common.
		</p>
		<p>
			The set of friends that you have in common is called the <term>intersection</term>. The intersection of two sets contains only the elements that are in both sets. An element must be in both set <m>A</m> <alert>and</alert> set <m>B</m> to be in their intersection.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-intersection">
	  	<idx>intersection</idx>
	  	<idx><h>sets</h><h>intersection</h></idx>
	  	<statement>
	  		<p>
					The <term>intersection</term> of two sets, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, denoted <m>A \cap B</m>, is the set containing all elements that are in both <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.  That is,
					<md>
						A \cap B = \{x\mid x\in A \text{ AND } x\in B \}.
					</md>
				</p>
	  	</statement>
	  </definition>
		<p>
			The set of all friends that you and your roommate have invited is called the <term>union</term>. The union of two sets contains all the elements contained in either set (or both). To be in the union of set <m>A</m> and set <m>B</m>, an element must be contained in just set <m>A</m>, just set <m>B</m>, <alert>or</alert> in the intersection of sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>. Notice that in this case the <q>or</q> is <alert>inclusive</alert>.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-union">
	  	<idx>union</idx>
	  	<idx><h>sets</h><h>union</h></idx>
	  	<statement>
	  		<p>
					The <term>union</term> of two sets, <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, denoted <m>A\cup B</m>, is the set containing all elements that are in <m>A</m> or in <m>B</m> (or both). That is,
					<md>
						A \cup B = \{x\mid x\in A \text{ OR } x\in B \}.
					</md>
				</p>
	  	</statement>
	  </definition>
		<p>
			What about the people who were <em>not</em> invited to the party and showed up anyway? They are not elements of your set of invited friends. Nor are they elements of your roommate's set of invited friends. These uninvited party crashers are in the <term>complement</term> of the set of all invited friends. The complement of a set <m>A</m> contains everything that is <em>not</em> in the set <m>A</m>. To be in the complement of set <m>A</m>, an element <alert>cannot</alert> be in set <m>A</m>, but it will be an element of the universal set.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-complement">
  		<idx>complement</idx>
  		<idx><h>sets</h><h>complement</h></idx>
  		<statement>
  			<p>
					The <term>complement</term> of a set <m>A</m> in a universal set <m>U</m> is the set <m>A^C</m> consisting of all elements in <m>U</m> that are not in <m>A</m>. That is,
					<md>
						A^{C} = \{x\in U\mid x \notin A \}.
					</md>
				</p>
				<p>
					<alert>Note:</alert> The complement of a set <m>A</m> may also be denoted by <m>A'</m> or <m>\overline{A}</m>.
				</p>
  		</statement>
  	</definition>
		<example>
			<statement>
			<p>
				Consider the sets: <m>A = \{\text{red, green, blue}\}</m>, <m>B = \{\text{red, yellow, orange}\}</m>, and <m>C = \{\text{red, orange, yellow, green, blue, indigo, violet}\}</m>.
				<ol marker="a)">
					<li>
						<p>
							Determine <m>A \cap B </m>, and write it in set notation.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Determine <m>A \cup B</m>, and write it in set notation.
						</p>
					</li>
					<li>
						<p>
							Determine <m>A^C \cap C</m> and write it in set notation.
						</p>
					</li>
				</ol>
			</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								The intersection contains the elements in both sets: <m>A \cap B = \{\text{red}\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								The union contains all the elements in either set: <m>A \cup B = \{\text{red, green, blue, yellow, orange}\}</m>. Notice we only list red once.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Here we are looking for all the elements that are <em>not</em> in set <m>A</m> and are in set <m>C</m>: <m>A^C \cap C = \{\text{orange, yellow, indigo, violet}\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>
	<subsection xml:id="subsec-sets-venn-diagrams">
		<title>Venn Diagrams</title>
		<p>
			<term>Venn diagrams</term> are used to illustrate the relationships between two or more sets. To create a Venn diagram, start by drawing a rectangle to represent the universal set. Next draw and label overlapping circles to represent each of your sets. Most often there will be two or three sets illustrated in a Venn diagram. Finally, if elements are given, fill in each region with its corresponding elements. Venn diagrams are also a great way to illustrate intersections, unions, and complements of sets, as shown below.
		</p>
		<definition xml:id="def-venn-diagram">
	  	<idx>Venn diagram</idx>
	  	<idx><h>sets</h><h>Venn diagram</h></idx>
	  	<statement>
	  		<p>
					A <term>Venn diagram</term> is a picture used to represent sets and their relationships.  Each set is represented by a circle, with the area inside the circle representing the elements that are in the set.  The entire diagram is enclosed in a rectangle representing the set of all possible elements, called the <term>universal set</term>.
				</p>
	  	</statement>
	  </definition>
		<sidebyside widths="33% 33% 33%">
			<figure>
				<caption>The <alert>intersection</alert> is the shaded region where the two sets overlap. It contains the elements in <m>A</m> <alert>and</alert> <m>B</m>.</caption>
				<image xml:id="img-set-intersection" width="100%">
				<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of two overlapping sets A and B inside a rectangle labeled U; only the region where the two circles overlap is shaded, showing the intersection.</shortdescription>
				<latex-image>
					\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
						% Define the universe as a rectangle
						\draw[thin] (0,0) rectangle (2.625,2);
						% Draw A
						\draw[fill=white] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
						% Draw B
						\draw[fill=white] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
						% Intersection area
						\begin{scope}
								\clip (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
								\fill[yellow!70] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
						\end{scope}
				
						% Labels
						\node[align=center] at (0.6,1) {$A$};
						\node[align=center] at (2.025,1) {$B$};
						\node[align=center] at (2.35,0.25) {$U$};
				
						% Draw borders
						\draw[draw=black, thin] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
						\draw[draw=black, thin] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
					\end{tikzpicture}
				</latex-image>
				</image>
      </figure>
			<figure>
				<caption>The <alert>union</alert> includes all elements in <m>A</m> <alert>or</alert> <m>B</m> or both. It is the entire shaded region.</caption>
				<image xml:id="img-set-union" width="100%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of two overlapping sets A and B inside a rectangle labeled U; both circles, including their overlap, are shaded to show the union.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
							% Define the universe as a rectangle
							\draw[thin] (0,0) rectangle (2.625,2);
							% Draw A
							\draw[fill=yellow!70] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
							% Draw B
							\draw[fill=yellow!70] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
							% Labels
							\node[align=center] at (0.6,1) {$A$};
							\node[align=center] at (2.025,1) {$B$};
							\node[align=center] at (2.35,0.25) {$U$};
					
							% Draw borders
							\draw[draw=black, thin] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
							\draw[draw=black, thin] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</figure>
			<figure>
				<caption>The <alert>complement</alert> of set <m>A</m> includes all the elements <alert>not</alert> in <m>A</m>. It is the shaded region outside <m>A</m>, but within the universal set.</caption>
				<image xml:id="img-set-complement" width="100%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram with only one set A; The region outside of circle A but inside the rectangle is shaded to represent the complement of the set, or elements not in set A.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
							% Define the universe as a rectangle
							\draw[thin, fill=yellow!70] (0,0) rectangle (2.625,2);
							% Draw A
							\filldraw[fill=white, draw=black, thin] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
							% labels
							\node[align=center] at (0.875,1) {$A$};
							\node[align=center] at (2.125,1) {$A^C$};
							\node[align=center] at (2.35,0.25) {$U$};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</figure>
	  </sidebyside>
		<example xml:id="ex-venn-diagram-construction">
			<title>Constructing a Venn Diagram</title>	  	
			<statement>
				<p>Let <m>A = \lbrace a, c, d, e \rbrace</m> and <m>B = \lbrace d, e, f \rbrace</m> be subsets of the universal set <m>U = \lbrace a, b, c, d, e, f \rbrace</m>.  Draw a Venn diagram representing these sets.</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>Draw a box to represent <m>U</m> and one circle for each of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, making sure that there is a region inside both circles.  Then place the elements in the appropriate region as shown below.</p>
				<figure xml:id="fig-ex-venn-diagram-const-letters">
					<caption>Venn diagram of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> in <m>U</m>.</caption>
					<image width="45%" xml:id="img-ex-venn-diagram-const-letters">
						<shortdescription>
							A Venn diagram with two circles, one for A and one for B, which overlap in the middle.  Elements a and c are in circle A but not circle B, element f is in circle B but not circle A, elements d and e are in both circles, and element b is not in either circle.
						</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
								\draw[fill=white, thin] (-1.5,-1.5) rectangle (2.5,1.5);
								\draw[fill=white, thin] (0,0) circle (1);
								\draw[fill=white, thin] (1,0) circle (1);
								\draw (0,0) circle (1); % outline
								\node at (-1,1) {$A$};
								\node at (2,1) {$B$};							  
								\node at (0.5,0.35)  {\small $d$};
								\node at (0.5,-0.35) {\small $e$};
								\node at (-0.5,0.1)  {\small $a$};
								\node at (-0.3,-0.3) {\small $c$};
								\node at (1.4,0)     {\small $f$};
								\node at (2,-1.2)  {\small $b$};
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
					</image>
				</figure>
			</solution>
		</example>
		<example>
			<statement>
				<p>
					Create a Venn diagram to illustrate <m>A^C \cap B</m>.
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					The set <m>A^C</m> contains all elements not in set <m>A</m>. This means that <m>A^C \cap B</m> contains elements that are not in <m>A</m> and are in <m>B</m>. That is, it contains the elements of <m>B</m> that are not in <m>A</m> as shown in the following Venn diagram.
				</p>
				<figure xml:id="fig-venn-ex-with-complement">
					<caption>A Venn diagram illustrating the set <m>A^C \cap B</m> shaded in yellow.</caption>
					<image width="45%" xml:id="img-venn-ex-with-complement">
						<shortdescription>
							A Venn diagram with two circles, one for A and one for B, which overlap in the middle.  The shaded region is a crescent shape in B that is not in A.
						</shortdescription>
						<latex-image>
							\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
								% Define the universe as a rectangle
								\draw[thin,fill=white] (0,0) rectangle (2.625,2);
								% Draw A
								\draw[fill=white] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
								% Draw B
								\draw[fill=white] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
								% A complement intersect B area
								\begin{scope}
										\clip (0,0) rectangle (2.625,2);
										\fill[yellow!70] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
										\begin{scope}
												\clip (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
												\fill[white] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
										\end{scope}
								\end{scope}
						
								% Labels
								\node[align=center] at (0.6,1) {$A$};
								\node[align=center] at (2.025,1) {$B$};
								\node[align=center] at (2.35,0.25) {$U$};
						
								% Draw borders
								\draw[draw=black, thin] (0.875,1) circle (0.75);
								\draw[draw=black, thin] (1.75,1) circle (0.75);
							\end{tikzpicture}
						</latex-image>
					</image>
				</figure>
			</solution>
		</example>
	</subsection>

	<exercises>
		<!-- Sources: Exercise 1 is adapted from Benjamin Jackson's MATH 105
		warm-up (Objective 9), Walla Walla University, Fall 2026. Exercises 4,
		5, 8, 10, and 16 are adapted from his MATH 105 lecture slides and
		clicker questions (Objective 9, Fall 2026). Exercises 9, 13, 14, 15,
		and 17 are based on his MATH 105 written-homework question library
		(Fall 2026). Exercises 2, 3, 6, 7, 11, and 12 are new.
		-->
		<title>Exercises</title>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-party-lists"><!--  #1 (Obj 9 warm-up: the party lists) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Mia and Noah are throwing a party. Their friend group is Ava, Ben, Cal, Dev, Eli, Fay, Gus, Hana, Ivy, and Jo. Mia's guest list is Ava, Cal, Dev, Fay, Cal, Ivy, and Noah's guest list is Ben, Cal, Fay, Gus, Ivy. Let <m>M</m> be the set of people on Mia's list and <m>N</m> the set of people on Noah's list.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Mia rewrites her list as Ivy, Fay, Dev, Cal, Ava. Is this the same guest list as before? Explain using sets.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Noah says that everyone in town who isn't on either list was not invited. Mia says that only Eli, Hana, and Jo were not invited. Who is right? Explain what the universal set has to do with the answer.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Using the friend group as the universal set, draw a Venn diagram that shows each of the ten friends exactly once.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								List the elements of <m>M \cap N</m>, <m>M \cup N</m>, and <m>(M \cup N)^C</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Yes. Both lists give the set <m>M = \{\text{Ava, Cal, Dev, Fay, Ivy}\}</m>. The order of the elements doesn't matter, and listing Cal twice doesn't invite him twice, because the elements of a set are distinct.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Both could be right; it depends on the universal set. If the universal set is everyone in town, Noah is right. If the universal set is the friend group, the people not invited are <m>\{\text{Eli, Hana, Jo}\}</m>, and Mia is right. The complement of a set depends on the universal set, which the context has to decide.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								See the diagram below.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>M \cap N = \{\text{Cal, Fay, Ivy}\}</m>, <m>M \cup N = \{\text{Ava, Ben, Cal, Dev, Fay, Gus, Ivy}\}</m>, and <m>(M \cup N)^C = \{\text{Eli, Hana, Jo}\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-party-lists" width="60%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains two overlapping circles labeled Mia and Noah. Mia only: Ava and Dev. Both: Cal, Fay, and Ivy. Noah only: Ben and Gus. Outside both circles: Eli, Hana, and Jo.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
							\draw (0,0) rectangle (6.8,3.3);
							\draw (2.2,1.45) circle (1.3);
							\draw (3.7,1.45) circle (1.3);
							\node at (1.5,3.02) {Mia};
							\node at (4.4,3.02) {Noah};
							\node[align=center] at (1.5,1.45) {Ava\\Dev};
							\node[align=center] at (2.95,1.45) {Cal\\Fay\\Ivy};
							\node[align=center] at (4.4,1.45) {Ben\\Gus};
							\node[align=center] at (5.9,1.1) {Eli\\Hana\\Jo};
							\node at (6.5,3.0) {$U$};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-roster-set-builder"><!--  #2 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write the sets in (a) through (c) in roster notation, and write the sets in (d) and (e) in set-builder notation.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{x \mid x \text{ is an odd digit}\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{x \mid x \text{ is a letter in the word Mississippi}\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{x \mid x \text{ is a month whose name begins with J}\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{\text{Saturday, Sunday}\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{1, 4, 9, 16, 25\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Answers to (d) and (e) may vary.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{1, 3, 5, 7, 9\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{\text{m, i, s, p}\}</m>. Each letter is listed only once, no matter how many times it appears in the word.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{\text{January, June, July}\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{x \mid x \text{ is a day of the weekend}\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{x \mid x \text{ is a perfect square from 1 to 25}\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-element-subset"><!--  #3 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>V = \{\text{a, e, i, o, u}\}</m>. Decide whether each statement is true or false.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\text{e} \in V</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\text{y} \in V</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{\text{a, e}\} \subseteq V</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>V \subseteq \{\text{a, e, i, o, u, y}\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{\text{a, e, y}\} \subseteq V</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\emptyset \subseteq V</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								True.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								False; y is not an element of <m>V</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								True; both a and e are elements of <m>V</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								True; every element of <m>V</m> is in the larger set.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								False; <m>\text{y} \notin V</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								True; the empty set is a subset of every set.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-subset-equal"><!--  #4 (Obj 9 clicker: subset, new sets) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Consider the sets
					<md>
						<mrow>A = \{2, 4, 6, 8, 10\}, \quad B = \{x \mid x \text{ is an even number from 2 to 10}\},</mrow>
						<mrow>C = \{2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12\}, \quad D = \{10, 8, 6, 4, 2, 2\}.</mrow>
					</md>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Is <m>A \subseteq C</m>? Is <m>C \subseteq A</m>?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Is <m>A \subseteq B</m>? Is <m>B \subseteq A</m>? What can you conclude?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Is <m>D = A</m>?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Jordan says, <q><m>A</m> can't be a subset of <m>B</m>, because they have the same elements. A subset has to be smaller.</q> Is Jordan right?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Yes, <m>A \subseteq C</m>, since every element of <m>A</m> is in <m>C</m>. No, <m>C</m> is not a subset of <m>A</m>, since <m>12 \in C</m> but <m>12 \notin A</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Yes and yes: <m>B = \{2, 4, 6, 8, 10\}</m>. Since <m>A \subseteq B</m> and <m>B \subseteq A</m>, we conclude <m>A = B</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Yes. Order doesn't matter, and listing 2 twice doesn't change the set.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								No. <m>A</m> is a subset of <m>B</m> because every element of <m>A</m> is in <m>B</m>; nothing in the definition requires <m>A</m> to be smaller. In fact, every set is a subset of itself.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-count-subsets"><!--  #5 (Obj 9 clicker: number of subsets, new set) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								List every subset of <m>\{x, y, z\}</m>. How many are there?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Predict how many subsets a set with 4 elements has. Explain your reasoning.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								There are 8 subsets: <m>\emptyset</m>, <m>\{x\}</m>, <m>\{y\}</m>, <m>\{z\}</m>, <m>\{x, y\}</m>, <m>\{x, z\}</m>, <m>\{y, z\}</m>, and <m>\{x, y, z\}</m>. Don't forget the empty set and the set itself.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								16. Each element is either in a subset or out of it, so adding a fourth element doubles the count: every subset of <m>\{x, y, z\}</m> can appear with or without the new element. A set with <m>n</m> elements has <m>2^n</m> subsets.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-empty-set"><!--  #6 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Which of the following sets are equal to the empty set <m>\emptyset</m>? Explain.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{0\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{x \mid x \text{ is a month with 32 days}\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{\emptyset\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>\{x \mid x \text{ is a whole number less than } 0\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Not empty: it has one element, the number 0.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Empty: no month has 32 days.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Not empty: it has one element, the empty set itself. Think of a bag holding an empty bag; the outer bag is not empty.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Empty: the whole numbers are <m>0, 1, 2, \ldots</m>, so none is less than 0.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-universal-set"><!--  #7 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					For each set in (a) through (c), suggest a reasonable universal set.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								The students in your math class who have a pet.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								The vowels.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								The songs on your playlist that are longer than four minutes.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Let <m>P</m> be the set in (a). Describe <m>P^C</m> when the universal set is your math class, and again when the universal set is every student at your college.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Answers may vary.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								All the students in your math class.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								The letters of the alphabet.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								All the songs on your playlist.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								With your math class as the universal set, <m>P^C</m> is your classmates who don't have a pet. With every student at your college as the universal set, <m>P^C</m> is every student at the college except the pet owners in your class, so it now includes everyone outside your class, pet owners or not.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-operations-numbers"><!--  #8 (Obj 9 clickers: intersection and complement, new sets) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>U = \{1, 2, 3, \ldots, 12\}</m>, let <m>A = \{1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12\}</m> be the divisors of 12, let <m>B = \{2, 4, 6, 8, 10, 12\}</m> be the even numbers in <m>U</m>, and let <m>C = \{1, 4, 9\}</m> be the perfect squares in <m>U</m>. List the elements of each set.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cap B</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cup C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>B^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(A \cup C)^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A^C \cap B</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cap B \cap C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cap B = \{2, 4, 6, 12\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cup C = \{1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>B^C = \{1, 3, 5, 7, 9, 11\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Parentheses first: <m>A \cup C = \{1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12\}</m>, so <m>(A \cup C)^C = \{5, 7, 8, 10, 11\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A^C = \{5, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11\}</m>, so <m>A^C \cap B = \{8, 10\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cap B = \{2, 4, 6, 12\}</m>, and the only one of these in <m>C</m> is 4, so <m>A \cap B \cap C = \{4\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-coffee-shop"><!--  #9 (Written 09a, new context) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Ana, Ben, and Cy work at the campus coffee shop. Let <m>U</m> be the set of days of the week, and let <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> be the days that Ana, Ben, and Cy work:
					<md>
						A = \{\text{Mon, Wed, Fri}\}, \quad B = \{\text{Mon, Tue, Sat}\}, \quad C = \{\text{Tue, Wed, Sun}\}.
					</md>
					List the elements of each set in (a) through (f).
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A^C \cap B^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(A \cup B)^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(A \cap B)^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A^C \cup B^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cup (B \cap C)</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(A \cup B) \cap (A \cup C)</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Which of your answers to (a) through (f) are equal?
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								On which days is neither Ana nor Ben working? Which of the sets above gives the answer?
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Here <m>A^C = \{\text{Tue, Thu, Sat, Sun}\}</m> and <m>B^C = \{\text{Wed, Thu, Fri, Sun}\}</m>.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A^C \cap B^C = \{\text{Thu, Sun}\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cup B = \{\text{Mon, Tue, Wed, Fri, Sat}\}</m>, so <m>(A \cup B)^C = \{\text{Thu, Sun}\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cap B = \{\text{Mon}\}</m>, so <m>(A \cap B)^C = \{\text{Tue, Wed, Thu, Fri, Sat, Sun}\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A^C \cup B^C = \{\text{Tue, Wed, Thu, Fri, Sat, Sun}\}</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>B \cap C = \{\text{Tue}\}</m>, so <m>A \cup (B \cap C) = \{\text{Mon, Tue, Wed, Fri}\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cup B = \{\text{Mon, Tue, Wed, Fri, Sat}\}</m> and <m>A \cup C = \{\text{Mon, Tue, Wed, Fri, Sun}\}</m>, so <m>(A \cup B) \cap (A \cup C) = \{\text{Mon, Tue, Wed, Fri}\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								(a) and (b) are equal, (c) and (d) are equal, and (e) and (f) are equal. The first two pairs are examples of De Morgan's laws for sets (see <xref ref="exer-sets-demorgan"/>), and the last pair is an example of a distributive law (see <xref ref="exer-sets-distributive"/>).
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Thursday and Sunday. These are the days that are not in <m>A \cup B</m>, so the answer is <m>(A \cup B)^C</m>, or equivalently <m>A^C \cap B^C</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-demorgan"><!--  #10 (Obj 9 clicker: De Morgan's principle, the other law) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Draw a Venn diagram of two sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, and shade the region for <m>(A \cap B)^C</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								On a second diagram, shade <m>A^C \cup B^C</m>. (Think of <m>A^C</m> and <m>B^C</m> separately, then shade everything in either one.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								On a third diagram, shade <m>A^C \cap B^C</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Which two of your diagrams show the same region? Using what you found, and <xref ref="exer-sets-coffee-shop"/> if you did it, complete these two statements, called <term>De Morgan's laws</term><idx>De Morgan's laws</idx><idx><h>sets</h><h>De Morgan's laws</h></idx> for sets: <m>(A \cap B)^C = </m> ____ and <m>(A \cup B)^C = </m> ____. Compare them with De Morgan's laws for logic in <xref ref="sec-logic-equivalence"/>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="0%" valign="top">
					<stack>
						<p>
							a) <m>(A \cap B)^C</m>
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-demorgan-a">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of two overlapping sets A and B inside a rectangle labeled U. Everything except the overlap of the two circles is shaded.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram2sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillNotAorNotB
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram2sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>
							b) <m>A^C \cup B^C</m>
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-demorgan-b">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of two overlapping sets A and B inside a rectangle labeled U. Everything except the overlap of the two circles is shaded, the same region as in part a.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram2sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillNotA\fillNotB
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram2sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>
							c) <m>A^C \cap B^C</m>
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-demorgan-c">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of two overlapping sets A and B inside a rectangle labeled U. Only the region outside both circles is shaded.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram2sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillNotAorB
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram2sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
				</sidebyside>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Everything except the overlap of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> is shaded.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A^C</m> is everything outside <m>A</m> and <m>B^C</m> is everything outside <m>B</m>. Together they cover everything except the overlap, the same region as in (a).
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Only the region outside both circles is shaded twice, so only it is in <m>A^C \cap B^C</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Diagrams (a) and (b) match, so <m>(A \cap B)^C = A^C \cup B^C</m>. Diagram (c) is the region outside both circles, which is the complement of <m>A \cup B</m>, so <m>(A \cup B)^C = A^C \cap B^C</m>. These match De Morgan's laws for logic, <m>\neg(p \land q) \equiv \neg p \lor \neg q</m> and <m>\neg(p \lor q) \equiv \neg p \land \neg q</m>, with complement in place of <q>not,</q> intersection in place of <q>and,</q> and union in place of <q>or.</q>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-venn-primes-odds"><!--  #11 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>U = \{1, 2, 3, \ldots, 10\}</m>, let <m>A</m> be the set of prime numbers in <m>U</m>, and let <m>B</m> be the set of odd numbers in <m>U</m>.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								List the elements of <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>, and draw a Venn diagram that places each element of <m>U</m> in the correct region.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Use your diagram to list the elements of <m>A \cap B</m>, <m>A^C \cap B</m>, and <m>(A \cup B)^C</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A = \{2, 3, 5, 7\}</m> and <m>B = \{1, 3, 5, 7, 9\}</m>. (Remember that 1 is not prime.) See the diagram below.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cap B = \{3, 5, 7\}</m>, <m>A^C \cap B = \{1, 9\}</m>, and <m>(A \cup B)^C = \{4, 6, 8, 10\}</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
				<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-venn-primes-odds" width="50%">
					<shortdescription>A Venn diagram: a rectangle labeled U contains two overlapping circles labeled A and B. A only: 2. Both: 3, 5, and 7. B only: 1 and 9. Outside both circles: 4, 6, 8, and 10.</shortdescription>
					<latex-image>
						\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\figfont]
							\draw (0,0) rectangle (6.8,3.3);
							\draw (2.2,1.45) circle (1.3);
							\draw (3.7,1.45) circle (1.3);
							\node at (1.5,3.02) {$A$};
							\node at (4.4,3.02) {$B$};
							\node at (1.5,1.45) {2};
							\node[align=center] at (2.95,1.45) {3\\5\\7};
							\node[align=center] at (4.4,1.45) {1\\9};
							\node[align=center] at (5.9,1.1) {4\quad 6\\8\quad 10};
							\node at (6.5,3.0) {$U$};
						\end{tikzpicture}
					</latex-image>
				</image>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-shading-two"><!--  #12 (new) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Draw a Venn diagram of two sets <m>A</m> and <m>B</m> for each part, and shade the region that represents the given set. Then describe the region in words.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cap B^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(A \cap B) \cup (A \cup B)^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(A \cup B) \cap (A \cap B)^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="0%" valign="top">
					<stack>
						<p>
							a) <m>A \cap B^C</m>
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-shading-two-a">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of two overlapping sets A and B inside a rectangle labeled U. Only the part of circle A outside circle B is shaded.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram2sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillOnlyA
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram2sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>
							b) <m>(A \cap B) \cup (A \cup B)^C</m>
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-shading-two-b">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of two overlapping sets A and B inside a rectangle labeled U. The overlap of the two circles and the region outside both circles are shaded.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram2sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillACapB\fillNotAorB
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram2sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>
							c) <m>(A \cup B) \cap (A \cap B)^C</m>
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-shading-two-c">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of two overlapping sets A and B inside a rectangle labeled U. The part of A outside B and the part of B outside A are shaded; the overlap and the region outside both circles are not.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram2sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillOnlyA\fillOnlyB
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram2sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
				</sidebyside>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								The elements in <m>A</m> but not in <m>B</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								The elements in both sets or in neither set.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								The elements in exactly one of the two sets.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-shading-three"><!--  #13 (Written 09c, new expressions) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Draw a Venn diagram of three overlapping sets <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, and <m>C</m> for each part, and shade the region that represents the given set.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>B \cap (A \cup C)^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C) \cup (B \cap C)</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(A \cap B \cap C)^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="0%" valign="top">
					<stack>
						<p>
							a) <m>B \cap (A \cup C)^C</m>
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-shading-three-a">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of three overlapping circles A, B, and C in a rectangle labeled U. Only the part of B outside both A and C is shaded.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillOnlyB
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>
							b) <m>(A \cap B) \cup (A \cap C) \cup (B \cap C)</m>
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-shading-three-b">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of three overlapping circles A, B, and C in a rectangle labeled U. Every region where at least two circles overlap is shaded, including the center where all three overlap.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillACapB\fillACapC\fillBCapC
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>
							c) <m>(A \cap B \cap C)^C</m>
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-shading-three-c">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of three overlapping circles A, B, and C in a rectangle labeled U. Everything is shaded except the center region where all three circles overlap.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillNotABC\fillOnlyA\fillOnlyB\fillOnlyC\fillACapBNotC\fillACapCNotB\fillBCapCNotA
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
				</sidebyside>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(A \cup C)^C</m> is everything outside both <m>A</m> and <m>C</m>; the part of it inside <m>B</m> is the region in <m>B</m> only.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Each intersection is one of the overlaps of two circles, so the union is every region in at least two of the sets.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cap B \cap C</m> is the center region, so its complement is everything else, including the region outside all three circles.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-distributive"><!--  #14 (Written 09b, the other distributive law) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Use Venn diagrams to show that
					<md>
						A \cup (B \cap C) = (A \cup B) \cap (A \cup C).
					</md>
					This is one of the <term>distributive laws</term><idx><h>sets</h><h>distributive laws</h></idx> for sets. Shade the left side on one diagram and the right side on another, and check that the shaded regions match. (<xref ref="exer-sets-coffee-shop"/> checked this law with lists of elements.)
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Left side: <m>B \cap C</m> is the overlap of <m>B</m> and <m>C</m>; taking the union with <m>A</m> adds all of <m>A</m>. Right side: <m>A \cup B</m> and <m>A \cup C</m> are shown below; the region shaded in both is all of <m>A</m> together with the overlap of <m>B</m> and <m>C</m>. Both sides give the region in the third diagram.
				</p>
				<sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="0%" valign="top">
					<stack>
						<p>
							<m>A \cup B</m>
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-distributive-a">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of three overlapping circles A, B, and C in a rectangle labeled U. All of circles A and B are shaded.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillA\fillB
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>
							<m>A \cup C</m>
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-distributive-b">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of three overlapping circles A, B, and C in a rectangle labeled U. All of circles A and C are shaded.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillA\fillC
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>
							Both sides
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-distributive-c">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of three overlapping circles A, B, and C in a rectangle labeled U. All of circle A is shaded, along with the overlap of B and C.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillA\fillBCapC
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
				</sidebyside>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-name-region"><!--  #15 (Written 09d, new regions) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Write a set expression, using <m>A</m>, <m>B</m>, <m>C</m>, <m>\cup</m>, <m>\cap</m>, and <m>^C</m>, for the shaded region of each Venn diagram.
				</p>
				<sidebyside widths="30% 30% 30%" margins="0%" valign="top">
					<stack>
						<p>
							a)
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-name-region-a">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of three overlapping circles A, B, and C in a rectangle labeled U. All of circles A and B are shaded, including their overlaps with C. The part of C outside A and B, and the region outside all circles, are not shaded.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillA\fillB
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>
							b)
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-name-region-b">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of three overlapping circles A, B, and C in a rectangle labeled U. Everything outside circle B is shaded; all of circle B is unshaded.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillNotB
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
					<stack>
						<p>
							c)
						</p>
						<image xml:id="img-exer-sets-name-region-c">
							<shortdescription>A Venn diagram of three overlapping circles A, B, and C in a rectangle labeled U. All of circle C is shaded except the center region where all three circles overlap.</shortdescription>
							<latex-image>
								\begin{venndiagram3sets}[shade=yellow!70, tikzoptions={font=\figfont}]
									\fillOnlyC\fillACapCNotB\fillBCapCNotA
									\node[below left] at (venn top right) {$U$};
								\end{venndiagram3sets}
							</latex-image>
						</image>
					</stack>
				</sidebyside>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					Other equivalent answers are possible.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>A \cup B</m> (everything in <m>A</m> or <m>B</m>, whether or not it is in <m>C</m>).
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>B^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>C \cap (A \cap B)^C</m>, or equivalently <m>C \cap (A^C \cup B^C)</m>: everything in <m>C</m> except the part also in both <m>A</m> and <m>B</m>.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-verbal-campus"><!--  #16 (Obj 9 slides: verbal practice) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>U</m> be the set of all college students, <m>W</m> the set of students who work on campus, <m>V</m> the set of vegetarian students, and <m>F</m> the set of first-year students. Describe each set in words.
					<ol marker="a)" cols="3">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>W \cap V</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>W \cup V</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>W^C \cup F^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>W^C \cap V^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>F \cup V \cup W</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>F \cap V \cap W</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Vegetarian students who work on campus.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who work on campus or are vegetarian (or both).
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who don't work on campus or aren't first-year students; that is, everyone except first-year students who work on campus. (This set equals <m>(W \cap F)^C</m>.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who neither work on campus nor are vegetarian. (This set equals <m>(W \cup V)^C</m>.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who are at least one of these: a first-year student, a vegetarian, or a campus worker.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Vegetarian first-year students who work on campus.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>

		<exercise xml:id="exer-sets-verbal-high-school"><!--  #17 (Written 09e, new context) -->
			<statement>
				<p>
					Let <m>U</m> be the set of students at a high school, <m>S</m> the set of students who play a sport, <m>M</m> the set of students in a music ensemble, and <m>J</m> the set of students who have a part-time job. Describe the sets in (a) through (f) in words, and write the sets in (g) and (h) in set notation.
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>S \cap M</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>M \cup J</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>J^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>S \cap J^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>(S \cup M)^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>S \cap M \cap J</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who are in a music ensemble but don't play a sport.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who have a part-time job but neither play a sport nor are in a music ensemble.
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</statement>
			<solution>
				<p>
					<ol marker="a)">
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who play a sport and are in a music ensemble.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who are in a music ensemble or have a part-time job (or both).
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who don't have a part-time job.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who play a sport but don't have a part-time job.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who neither play a sport nor are in a music ensemble. (This set equals <m>S^C \cap M^C</m>.)
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								Students who play a sport, are in a music ensemble, and have a part-time job.
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>M \cap S^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
						<li>
							<p>
								<m>J \cap (S \cup M)^C</m>, or equivalently <m>J \cap S^C \cap M^C</m>
							</p>
						</li>
					</ol>
				</p>
			</solution>
		</exercise>
	</exercises>
</section>